In Fig. 3c we provide an early demonstration of a more exotic kind of conditioning in which we attempted to solve for backbone configurations subjected to arbitrary volumetric shape specifications. We accomplished this by adding heuristic classifier gradients based on optimal transport distances 48 between atoms in the structures and user-provided point clouds (Supplementary Appendix R). As a stress test of this capability, we conditioned the generation of single protein chains on the shapes of the Latin alphabet and Arabic numerals (Supplementary Fig. 18 and Supplementary Appendix K.3). We see the model routinely implementing several core phenomena of protein backbones, such as high secondary-structure content, close packing with room for designed side chains, and volume-spanning α-helical bundle and β-sheet elements. Although these shapes represent purely a challenging set of test geometries, more generally shape is intimately related to function in biology, for example, with membrane transporters, receptors and structured assemblies that organize molecular events in space. Being able to control shape would be a useful subroutine for generalized programmable protein engineering.
